# Ab Initio Study of Hydrogen Adsorption in MOF-5

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising adsorbents for hydrogen storage. Density functional theory and second-order Møller-Plesset perturbation theory (MP2) are used to calculate the interaction energies between H(2) and individual structural elements of the MOF-5 framework. The strongest interaction, DeltaH(77) = -7.1 kJ/mol, is found for the alpha-site of the OZn(4)(O(2)Ph)(6) nodes. We show that dispersion interactions and zero-point vibrational energies must be taken into account. Comparison of calculations done under periodic boundary conditions for the complete structure with those done for finite models cut from the MOF-5 framework shows that the interactions with H(2) originate mainly from the local environment around the adsorption site. When used within a Multi-Langmuir model, the MP2 results reproduce measured adsorption isotherms (the predicted amount is 6 wt % at 77 K and 40 bar) if we assume that the H(2) molecules preserve their rotational degrees of freedom in the adsorbed state. This allows to discriminate between different isotherms measured for different MOF-5 samples and to reliably predict isotherms for new MOF structures.

Institut f¨ur Chemie, Humboldt-UniVersita¨t zu Berlin, Berlin, Germany, and Cluster of Excellence, UNICAT, Berlin
## Introduction
The world’s increasing demand of energy, depletion of fossil fuels, and environmental concerns necessitate the quest for alternative solutions in current energy production, transfer, and utilization. The change to pollutant-free and more efficient energy exploitation can be achieved with the use of hydrogen as an intermediate medium for storing and transporting energy that is produced from any (preferably renewable) primary source. To meet customer requirements in road transport, the U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) has specified that the on-board hydrogen storage system should store 6 and 9 wt $\%$ of hydrogen by the year 2010 and 2015, respectively. Among many materials considered for hydrogen storage, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) show great potential to fulfill those requirements: an exceptionally high hydrogen uptake of MOF-5 up to 7.1 wt $\%$ at approximately 40 bar and $77\mathrm{~K~}$ has been determined.1 Moreover, a neutron powder diffraction study2 has shown that the MOF-5 host lattice has enough space to hold hydrogen molecules up to 11 wt $\%$ at $3.5\mathrm{~K~}$ (a pressure of 170 bar is required to reach this loading at $77~\mathrm{K}$ ).1 At room temperature, the maximum hydrogen storage capacity is much lower, in the case of MOF-5 between 0.2 and 0.45 wt $\%.^{3-6}$ The low hydrogen adsorption at room temperature is attributable to the weak interaction between MOF-5 and hydrogen; the measured hydrogen adsorption enthalpies are ranging from $-3.5$ to $-5.2$ $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ .7-9 To design MOF-based materials with high hydrogen storage capacities at room temperature (with adsorption enthalpies between $-15$ and $-40\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol})^{10-12}$ detailed understanding of the interactions between MOFs and hydrogen molecules at molecular level is needed.
On the basis of accurate calculations of adsorption energies, molecular statistics, and a Multi-Langmuir adsorption model we will show that reliable ab initio predictions of adsorption isotherms are possible. Even for well-known systems like $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ such predictions can help to discriminate between different measurements that yield varying adsorption isotherms.1,4,5,13-16
Hydrogen adsorption in MOFs has been studied before with different computational methods.7,17-24 Density functional theory (DFT) can treat periodic MOF structures and, hence, include long-range Coulombic interactions, but most of the currently available functionals do not properly take dispersion forces into account. The latter can be included by adding a semiempirical $1/r^{6}$ term to DFT energies $(\mathrm{DFT+D})^{25,26}$ or by using post Hartree-Fock methods that describe electron correlation explicitly. Because the use of even the simplest of the latter, second-order Møller-Plesset perturbation theory (MP2), is far beyond present computational resources for periodic structures with large unit cells,,28 such calculations are performed only on finite-sized models that represent individual structural elements of the whole MOF framework. So far, relatively small models have been used,7,21,22,24,29,30 and thus, the effect of increasing model size on the adsorption energies is not known. Also, when even large, localized basis sets with, e.g., diffuse functions are applied, superposition errors (BSSE) are unavoidable, and counterpoise (CP) corrections31 must be evaluated, preferably with extrapolation to the complete basis set (CBS) limit. Usually this is not done, and this is the reason why calculated hydrogen adsorption energies, which are of the order of $6~\mathrm{{kJ/mol}}$ , vary over more than $3\mathrm{\kJ/mol.}^{7,22}$ Apparent agreement of such results with measured adsorption enthalpies is also questionable because the calculations report energies, whereas experimental results are enthalpies that include zeropoint vibrational energy changes and thermal corrections. Furthermore, experiments yield average values because different sites with different adsorption enthalpies are partially populated at a given loading.
The latter effect can be modeled with Monte Carlo or molecular dynamics methods,20,23,32 but this requires millions of energy calculations which is only possible if parametrized potentials (force fields) are used. The need for parametrization limits the predictive value of this approach and may lead to deviations of the calculated isotherms from observed ones.32
Our approach relies on a Multi-Langmuir model which combines adsorption isotherms for individual adsorption sites into an overall isotherm. The free energies for the individual sites are calculated within the rigid rotor, harmonic oscillator, and ideal gas approximation. This makes it possible to determine the contribution of each specific site to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of MOF-5 at different temperatures and pressures. The Multi-Langmuir model is applicable because the interactions between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules in different adsorption sites and between an $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule in one of these sites and in a possible “second layer” are much weaker (below $0.5\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ ) than the binding of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the adsorption sites considered (between 5 and $8~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\$ ). Another condition is that framework structure and adsorption volume do not change with the loading, a phenomenon that is observed in cases like MIL-53(Cr) which require different simulation techniques.33
For the adsorption energies of the individual sites a hybrid MP2(finite model):DFT(periodic structure) approach is available34,35 that describes the full crystal lattice with DFT applying periodic boundary conditions and the adsorption site with accurate electron correlation methods. For adsorption and reactions in microporous zeolites where dispersion forces play also an important role this hybrid MP2:DFT scheme yielded significantly more reliable results than periodic DFT or electron correlation calculations on finite-sized models alone.35,36
In this study we use DFT on periodic structures and MP2 on finite models to calculate the interaction energy between hydrogen and the different adsorption sites in MOF-5 (comparison is also made with the $\mathrm{\DeltaDFT+D}$ approach). The chosen models turn out large enough that the contributions from the periodic lattice to the interaction energies can be neglected. We also conclude that reliable energies for the individual sites can be obtained from counterpoise corrected MP2/aug-cc-pVTZ results.
## Models
MOF-5 is also known as IRMOF-1 because it is the first member of a series of isoreticular (cubic) metal-organic frameworks $\left(\mathrm{IRMOF}\right)^{37-39}$ with oxide-centered $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ tetrahedra as nodes linked by organic molecules. In MOF-5 (Figure 1) the organic linker is 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (BDC). Note that, depending on the synthesis process, residual $\mathrm{znO}$ species in the pores and lattice interpenetration accompanied by a change of crystal symmetry from cubic to trigonal may have changed the adsorption properties.40
Here, we refer to the high crystalline phase with a high specific surface area. Its primitive cell includes two nodes and six linker molecules, corresponding to two $\mathrm{OZn}_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ formula units. The conventional cell $F\bar{m3}m$ symmetry) is cubic and includes eight formula units. Figure 1 shows the location of the studied adsorption sites. Previously the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site has been referred to as Cup site, the $\beta$ -site as $\mathrm{ZnO}_{3}$ site, the $\gamma$ -site as $\mathrm{ZnO}_{2}$ site, and the Linker site as Hex site.2 There are four $\upalpha.$ - and four $\beta$ -sites, twelve $\gamma$ -sites, and six Linker sites per $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ formula unit, and the full occupation of these sites would represent a full “monolayer” of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules adsorbed on the “internal” surface of MOF-5.
In this work, in addition to the periodic structures, finitesized models cut out from the MOF-5 framework are studied (Figure 2). For the $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ nodes of the framework two models of different sizes are used. The smaller one consists of an oxidecentered $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ tetrahedron with six formate anions as ligands at the edges of the tetrahedron, $\mathrm{OZn_{4}(C O_{2}H)_{6}}$ . In the bigger model the six ligands are benzoate instead of formate anions, $\mathrm{OZn_{4}(C O_{2}P h)_{6}}$ . The small and big models will be referred to as formate model and benzoate model, respectively. For the organic part of the framework (linker) a model is used that consists of two formate models connected by a benzene ring.
## Methods
DFT Calculations. Were performed with the PerdewBurke-Ernzerhofer (PBE) exchange-correlation functional employing the $\mathrm{VASP^{41,42}}$ program package. Valence electrons were described by a plane wave basis set with a kinetic energy cutoff of $400\mathrm{eV}$ , and the core, by projector augmented waves. The Brillouin zone was sampled with a $2\times2\times2$ Monkhorst-Pack $\mathbf{k}$ -point mesh.43 For the PBE calculations with periodic boundary conditions the equilibrium volume of the primitive cell was obtained by fitting a Murnaghan equation of state to a series of fixed-volume relaxed structures. The minimum energy is obtained for a lattice constant of the conventional unit cell $\begin{array}{r}{\ddot{a_{\mathrm{C}}}=26.131\ \mathring{\Delta}}\end{array}$ $F m\bar{3}m$ symmetry). This is in good agreement with lattice constant determined experimentally2 $(a_{\mathrm{C}}=25.91{\mathring{\mathrm{A}}})$ ) and obtained in previous theoretical
The PBE $^+$ Dispersion calculations under periodic boundary conditions were performed using the QMPOT program45-47 that combines PBE energy and force calculations with the dispersion term (“Grimme-2006” parameters)26 using an additional interface48
studies.18,44 In all following periodic DFT calculations this cell parameter was kept constant. For DFT calculations on finite models a cubic computational box with length of $25\textup{\AA}$ was used to avoid interactions between periodic images.
MP2 Calculations. Were performed with the ricc2 module49-51 of the TURBOMOLE V5.10 program package.52 Only the valence shell electrons were correlated (frozen core approximation), and the resolution of identity (RI) approximation also known as density fitting was employed.53 The def2-TZVP basis sets of Ahlrichs et al.54,55 and the correlation-consistent (aug-)- cc-pVXZ (where $X=\mathrm{T}$ and Q) basis sets by Dunning et al.56,57 were applied. For example, the contracted functions for def2- TZVP and aug-cc- $\cdot\mathrm{pVT}Z$ basis sests on $Z_{\mathrm{{n}/{O},C/H}}$ are [6s5p4d1f/ 5s3p2d1f/3s1p] and [6s6p5d3f2g/5s4p3d2f/4s3p2d], respectively. For Zn the (aug-)cc-pVXZ-PP basis $\sec^{58,59^{\prime}}$ is used with the Stuttgart-Koeln MCDHF RSC ECP.60 For RI calculations appropriate auxiliary basis functions were applied.55,61,62 The counterpoise scheme is applied to correct calculated adsorption energies for BSSE.31 BSSE-corrected MP2 adsorption energies calculated with Dunning’s basis sets for $X=\mathrm{~T~}$ and Q are extrapolated to the CBS limit. CBS(D,T) denotes the extrapolation from aug-cc-pVDZ and aug-cc-pVTZ basis sets. For the Hartree-Fock (HF) contribution to the MP2 adsorption energy an exponential scheme63,64 is chosen, whereas an inverse power $\mathrm{1aw}^{65}$ is applied to the correlation energy contribution.
Vibrational Frequencies. Were calculated within the harmonic approximation on fully MP2/def2-TZVP optimized finite-sized models. No scaling was applied. Calculations on formate models show that upon $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on the $\upalpha$ -site the vibrational frequencies of the model change only by $1.2\%$ on average. The largest changes are associated with the four frequencies below $50~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ among which one frequency is increased from 20 to $29~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . This does not change the calculated adsorption enthalpies but decreases the adsorption entropy by 7 J/K. For the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on $\beta$ - and $\gamma$ -sites the largest changes in the vibrational frequencies of the model do not exceed $6.5\%$ . Also, the stretching vibrational frequency of adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is the same when calculated with the full Hessian or using a partial Hessian considering only the 6 degrees of freedom of the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule. Thus, zero-point vibrational energies and thermal corrections for the adsorption enthalpies as well as free energies were obtained from partial Hessians calculated for the benzoate models.
Adsorption Isotherms. Were calculated according to the multisite Langmuir equation66 which yields the total surface coverage, $\theta$ , as the sum of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed on different sites.
$$
\theta=x_{\mathrm{{\scriptsize{R}}}}{\frac{K_{\mathrm{{\scriptsize{a}}}}P}{1+K_{\mathrm{{\scriptsize{a}}}}P}}+x_{\beta}{\frac{K_{\beta}P}{1+K_{\beta}P}}+x_{\mathrm{{\scriptsize{L}}}}{\frac{K_{\mathrm{{\scriptsize{L}}}}P}{1+K_{\mathrm{{\scriptsize{L}}}}P}}+\quad
$$
The last term in this equation gives the adsorption on $\gamma$ -sites and takes into account that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ can bind to $\gamma$ -sites only if $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is already adsorbed on $\mathfrak{a}$ -site first. The fraction of sites $x_{\mathrm{site}}=N_{\mathrm{site}}/N$ is the ratio of the number $N_{\mathrm{site}}$ of specific adsorption sites per $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ formula unit and the total number of sites per formula unit, $N=26$ .
$K$ is adsorption equilibrium constant
$$
K={\frac{Q^{\mathrm{surf}}}{Q^{\mathrm{gas}}}}{\mathrm{e}}^{-{\frac{D_{0}}{R T}}}
$$
where $Q^{\mathrm{surf}}$ and $\mathcal{Q}^{\mathrm{gas}}$ are the partition functions of hydrogen on the surface and in gas phase, respectively, and $D_{0}$ is interaction energy between hydrogen and the MOF surface at $_{0\mathrm{~K~}}$ , i.e. including the zero-point energy changes, $\Delta Z_{\mathrm{PE}}$ ,
$$
D_{0}=\Delta E+\Delta\mathrm{ZPE}
$$
where $\Delta E$ is the CP-corrected MP2/CBS(D,T) adsorption energy calculated on MP2/def2-TZVP optimized benzoate models.
The rotational partition function, $q_{\mathrm{rot}},$ is computed by direct summation of the first 50 rotational levels,67
$$
\begin{array}{r}{q_{\mathrm{rot}}=\displaystyle\frac{1}{4}\sum_{J\mathrm{even}}\left(2J+1\right)\mathrm{e}^{-\Theta_{\mathrm{rot}}J(J+1)/T}+\qquad}\\ {\displaystyle\frac{3}{4}\sum_{J\mathrm{odd}}\left(2J+1\right)\mathrm{e}^{-\Theta_{\mathrm{rot}}J(J+1)/T}}\end{array}
$$
where $J$ is the rotational quantum number and $\Theta_{r o t}$ is the rotational temperature.
## Results and Discussion
Comparison of Different Methods for Adsorption Energies and Structures. Previous studies have shown that MP2 adsorption energies are very dependent on the basis set applied and on the BSSE. For example, for hydrogen binding to the formate model, BSSE corrected adsorption energies range from $-3.1$ to $-5.1\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ ,7,29,30 whereas uncorrected adsorption energies can be as large as $-6.9\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ .22 Table 1 shows the MP2 results of this study with increasing basis set size. Without diffuse functions the BSSE can reach $50\%$ . Addition of diffuse functions (aug-cc-pVXZ) reduces the basis set dependence significantly and yields a complete basis set (CBS) limit of $-6.0\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ . The MP2/aug-cc-pVTZ result $(-5.4\mathrm{kJ/mol})$ is only $0.6\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ smaller than this limit.
Because calculations with quadruple- $\xi$ basis set are not feasible on benzoate models, we use in the following adsorption energies that are extrapolated to the CBS limit from BSSEcorrected MP2 adsorption energies calculated with aug-cc-pVDZ and aug-cc-pVTZ basis sets on MP2/def2-TZVP optimized structures. For the formate model, this CBS(D,T) adsorption energy $\left(-6.1~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\right)$ ) is very close to the value obtained from the extrapolation using the aug-cc-pVTZ and aug-cc-pVQZ basis sets on the MP2/aug-cc-pVTZ structure $\left(-6.0\mathrm{\kJ/mol}\right)$ . The estimates for the remaining uncertainties concerning neglect of higher-order electron correlation effects show slight increase in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy for the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site (about $0.4~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , see Supporting Information) which may be partially compensated by a small decrease of $0.1\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ due to larger basis sets (Table 1). For the Linker site the inclusion of higher excitations and the use of larger basis sets is expected21,24 to decrease the MP2 binding energy by about $0.75\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ . In total, these effects would change the amount of adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from 6.0 to 5.6 wt $\%$ at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 40 bar, respectively, indicating that the obtained adsorption energies and isotherms are likely to be very close to the converged results.
To investigate the effect of the model size on the hydrogen adsorption energies first DFT calculations were performed for a periodic structure in which hydrogen molecules occupy either all four $\mathfrak{a}$ -sites or all four $\beta$ -sites belonging to one of the two $\mathrm{OZn_{4}}$ nodes in the primitive cell (Table 2). This corresponds to loading of four $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules per $\mathrm{OZn_{4}(B D C)_{3}}$ formula unit. For the DFT structure single-point calculations are made on the formate and benzoate models, and the results are reported in Table 2. The PBE and $\mathrm{PBE^{+}}$ Dispersion results show that the adsorption energies are converged to the periodic value already for the benzoate model.
Comparison of DFT with DFT $^+$ Dispersion and of MP2 with Hartree-Fock (HF) indicates that dispersion is the major contribution to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding. Note, however, that PBE may effectively already include some dispersion in the region of overlapping densities68 and that the correlation part of the interaction energy, $\Delta E_{\mathrm{\mathrm{~cor}}}=\Delta E\left(\mathrm{MP2}\right)-\Delta E\left(\mathrm{HF}\right)$ , is dominated by dispersion, but also includes additional effects.69 Consistent with these findings, for the formate and benzoate models of the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site, $\Delta E_{\mathrm{\cor}}$ $-5.5$ and $-6.9$ , respectively) is larger than the dispersion correction to PBE $\cdot-3.7$ and - $\cdot4.5\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , respectively). The increase of these energies with increasing the model from formate to benzoate shows that dispersion interactions with the benzene rings makes a significant contribution to the total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies. For the formate and benzoate models of the $\beta$ -site, $\Delta E_{\mathrm{{cor}}}$ ( $-2.2$ and $-3.9\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , respectively) and the dispersion correction to PBE $_{-1.5}$ and $-\ 2.6\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , respectively) show the same trend, but are smaller. Since these interactions are missing in all DFT calculations even on periodic structures, this results in wrong relative stabilities of hydrogen adsorbed on different sites. According to periodic DFT $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ is more strongly (by $0.3\mathrm{kJ/mol}\$ ) bound to $\beta$ -sites than to $\mathfrak{a}$ -sites, whereas periodic DFT $+$ Dispersion and MP2 calculations show the opposite trend, binding on $\mathfrak{a}$ -sites is 1.4 and $2.1\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ stronger, respectively. Agreement between DFT+Dispersion results for the benzoate model and the periodic structure shows that it is mainly the local environment around the adsorption site that determines the adsorption properties of MOF-5 and modeling of hydrogen adsorption with relatively large, but finite models (Figure 2) is well justified.
From the results presented so far we conclude that MP2 calculations should be performed for the benzoate model of the nodes and the linker model and that the aug-ccpVTZ basis set should be used with CP corrections for the energies. A further question is which reference structure should be used. Structure optimizations at this level, as reported in Table 1 for the formate model, would be extremely costly for the benzoate models, but can be avoided. It turns out that the CP-MP2/aug-cc-pVTZ $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption energies ( $\mathrm{\bf\dot{a}}$ -site) for the formate model calculated at the MP2/def2-TZVP and MP2/aug-cc-pVTZ structures (cf. Table 1) are the same, both are $-5.4\mathrm{~kJ}/\mathrm{mol}$ . For the benzoate model, the CP-corrected MP2/aug-cc-pVTZ//MP2/def2-TZVP adsorption energy is $-7.6~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ .
Table 3presents MP2/def2-TZVP structure data for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on the different sites for the benzoate models. Because the interactions are stronger, the distances between adsorbed hydrogen and the MOF surface is $5~\mathrm{pm}$ shorter $a-$ site) than for the formate model and the $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{H}$ bond length of adsorbed hydrogen is $0.1\ \mathrm{pm}$ longer. The calculated harmonic frequencies of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed on formate and benzoate models are 4511 and $4494~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , respectively. The calculated frequency shift upon adsorption is $-\hat{5}0~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ (Table 3) relative to the harmonic frequency of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the gas phase $(4545~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})_{\cdot}$ ). The
IR spectra recorded by Vitillo et al.7,70 show indeed an absorption maximum at $4112~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ which corresponds to frequency shift of $-49~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ relative to para-hydrogen in gas phase. Also the heat of adsorption of $-7.4\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ derived from intensity changes with the temperature is in good agreement with the $\Delta H_{77}=-7.1\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ $\Delta E=-8.0\mathrm{kJ/mol},$ ) we calculate for the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site (Table 4). A recent diffuse reflectance IR study71 does not reveal directly vibrational transitions at this energy, but a shift of $\Delta\omega(\mathrm{H{-}\dot{H})=-49.3~c m^{-1}}$ can be derived from the S(0) rovibrational band at $4448.5~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . This confirms that our calculations reproduce well experimentally measured frequency shifts of hydrogen molecule upon adsorption. The calculated frequency shifts of hydrogen adsorbed on all sites studied are also presented in Table 3.
MP2 Results for Use with Multi-Langmuir Description of Adsorption. Table 4shows CP-corrected MP2/CBS(D,T)//MP2/ def2-TZVP energies for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on all sites using the benzoate models. Figure 3 shows the structure of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in a $\gamma$ -site next to an $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in an $\mathfrak{a}$ -site. If there were no $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule in the $\mathfrak{a}$ -position, a molecule in the less stable $\gamma$ -position $(-5.2\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , Table 4) would move into the more stable $\mathfrak{a}$ -position $(-8.0\ \mathrm{kJ}/\$ mol). The $\mathrm{H}_{2}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pair interaction in the complex is $-0.19\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ (CP-corr. MP2/CBS(D,T)/def2-TZVP), and the distance between the bond midpoints is $355~\mathrm{pm}$ . For two isolated $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules in the most stable T-shape configuration we get $-0.32\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol}$ at 368 pm, whereas the most accurate ab initio $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ potential yields $-0.48$ $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ at $335~\mathrm{pm}$ .72 Thus, it is the $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}5$ interaction that determines the position of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules. The very weak $\mathrm{H}_{2}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction compared to the strong $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}5$ interaction makes the Langmuir model work. Further evidence comes from the following comparison. Table 1 shows MP2/def2-TZVP binding energies for the $\upalpha{\cdot}$ -site (formate model). There are four $\mathfrak{a}$ -sites around such a model (which would belong to different MOF cavities). If this calculation is done for one $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule/three empty sites and for four $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules/no empty site, the average binding energies per $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule are $-5.18$ and $-5.15\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , respectively.
Upon adsorption the three translational and two rotational degrees of freedom of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule in the gas phase may be converted into five vibrations relative to the adsorption site. The corresponding harmonic frequencies are also presented in Table 3. These vibrations make substantial contributions to the free energy of adsorption as the data in the right part of Table 4 show. Comparison of experimentally measured1,14-16 and calculated adsorption isotherms in Figure 4 shows that adsorption isotherms derived from free energies for the adsorption sites calculated this way underestimate the amount of adsorbed hydrogen. However, experimental evidence shows that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules can retain both of their rotational degrees of freedom when weakly adsorbed in zeolites73,74 or other microporous frameworks.70,75 Recently, this was confirmed also for hydrogen adsorbed in MOF-5.71 If we assume that adsorbed hydrogen molecules have preserved their rotational degrees of freedom and that only translations are converted into vibrations on adsorption (see the data in the left part of Table 4), then the calculated adsorption isotherms are in very good agreement with experimental ones (Figure 4).
The free rotator-like behavior of the hydrogen molecule can be rationalized by the very flat potential energy surface at the adsorption site. For the almost spherical hydrogen molecule with a small moment of inertia (large rotational constant) this means that the orientation-dependent intermolecular interactions are quite small. The highest rotational barriers estimated from two different INS experiments76,77 are 0.6 and $1.8~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ .
The data presented in Table 4 make it possible to explain how retaining the rotational freedom of hydrogen molecules in the adsorbed state increases the predicted amount of adsorbed hydrogen. The adsorption enthalpies are larger (absolute terms) when hydrogen on the surface is treated as a free rotator. Of the differences, $70\mathrm{-}80\%$ correspond to the missing zero-point energies for the two vibrational modes that are substituted with rotations. FitzGerald et al.71 report that the zero-point energy associated with the center-of-mass translational motion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in MOF-5 is of the order of $1.5~\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ for three modes with a translational frequency of $84~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Our calculated values for three vibrational modes are between 1.1 and $2.0\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ .
Figure 5 shows how the different contributions to the adsorption enthalpy change with temperature taking $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ on the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site as example. The right part assumes that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ retains its two rotations on adsorption, whereas the left part assumes that rotations and translations are converted in 5 vibrations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ relative to the MOF surface. There are no rotational or translational contributions from the models for MOF-5 and $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdots}\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ because they are part of the rigid solid. The translations (right) or translations and rotations (left) of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the gas phase make a negative contribution to the adsorption enthalpy that grows linearly with temperature and is responsible for the initial decrease of the adsorption enthalpy (Figures 5, $\Delta H_{\mathrm{Trans,~Rot}})$ . The vibrational contribution, $\Delta H_{\mathrm{Vib}}$ , is positive, and its nonlinear growth causes the increase of the adsorption enthalpy above 133 and $100\mathrm{K}$ (right and left part of Figure 5, respectively). It is dominated by the three (right) or five (left) vibrational modes of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ relative to the MOF-5 surface. The contribution of the $_\mathrm{H-H}$ stretch mode to the adsorption enthalpy is constant below $400~\mathrm{K}$ .
When $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ retains its rotational degrees of freedom in the adsorbed state, $\Delta H_{\mathrm{Ads}}$ is more negative because $\Delta E_{\mathrm{ZPE}}$ is smaller, and it is less dependent on temperature (it goes from $-6.0$ at 0 $\mathrm{\DeltaK}$ to $-7.2$ at $133\mathrm{~K~}$ , and then slowly decreases, reaching $-6.8$ $\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ at $300~\mathrm{K}$ ).
Multi-Langmuir Adsorption Isotherms. Our calculated amount of adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ refers to excess adsorption which means that it is compared to the amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the gas phase at the same conditions. Our calculations treat $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the gas phase as ideal gas and, thus, fail at low temperatures and very high pressures where intermolecular interactions can not be neglected.
In the present study the maximum adsorption capacity is limited to about 6.8 wt $\%$ which is the maximum amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules that can adsorb on the adsorption sites considered. As can be seen from Figure 6, with exception to the abovementioned conditions, our Multi-Langmuir approach reproduces experimental isotherms at different temperatures and over wide pressure range. Comparison with isotherms measured by Zhou et al.78 give similar results. Furthermore, the present approach also works well for ${\bf D}_{2}$ : Comparison with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ isotherms measured by FitzGerald et al.71 shows an approximately $10\%$ larger molar adsorption. This is well reproduced by our calculated isotherms which predict adsorbed amounts of 8.0 and $8.8\mathrm{mmol/g}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf D}_{2}$ , respectively, at 1 bar and $77~\mathrm{K}$ , (Figure 4).
How sensitive are our adsorption results to a change of the input data for the individual sites? We have already mentioned that remaining uncertainties in the adsorption energies may change the amount $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed at $77~\mathrm{K}$ and 40 bar from 6.0 to 5.6 wt $\%$ . Scaling all vibrational frequencies by $10\%$ , much more than the expected uncertainty of the calculations reported in Table 3, will change the adsorbed amount from 6.0 to 6.2 wt $\%$ only.
The lower part of Figure 4 shows the population of different adsorption sites with increasing pressure at $77\mathrm{K}$ , which follows the order of increasing adsorption enthalpies. At low pressures the overall adsorption is determined by adsorption on $\mathtt{a}$ - sites. The fact that the $\mathfrak{a}$ -sites are populated first is also supported by single-crystal neutron diffraction79 and lowtemperature neutron powder diffraction on different loading.2,79
Furthermore, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy calculated for the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site $\left(-7.1~\mathrm{{\kJ/mol}}\right)$ is well comparable with the initial heat of adsorption close to $-7\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ measured by Mulder et al.77 and Dinca et al.80 at very low hydrogen loadings. Moreover, Bordiga et al.7 have observed sites in MOF-5 with adsorption enthalpies of $-7.4\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ . Upon adsorption on these sites the $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{H}$ frequency is shifted by $-49~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is in good correspondence with the $-50~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ calculated for the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site. Despite small differences in adsorption enthalpies (less than 0.5 $\mathrm{kJ/mol}\$ ), the coverage of the $\gamma$ -site increases faster because of the larger number of $\gamma$ -sites available. At a loading of four $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules per formula unit, there are three $\mathfrak{a}$ -sites and one $\beta$ -site occupied with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules. This is exactly what is witnessed in INS spectra.76 Our calculations show that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to $\gamma$ -sites is possible only after $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption on $\mathfrak{a}$ -sites because otherwise $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ moves always from the $\gamma$ -site to the more stable position on $\mathfrak{a}$ -site when there is no $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ on the $\mathfrak{a}$ -site to prevent this (Figure 3). Previous DFT study of hydrogen adsorption in MOF-5 failed in this respect because neglecting dispersion interactions results in a different stability order of different adsorption sites.18 On the whole, in accordance with numerous previous studies, we observe that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to the inorganic part of the framework is preferred.
## Conclusions
Dispersion is the major contribution binding $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ onto MOF5, and the adsorption properties are determined by the local environment around the binding sites. Consequently, the use of DFT with an improper description of dispersion or the use of models that are too small with methods that include dispersion do not result in converged adsorption energies. Ab initio quantum chemistry (second-order Møller-Plesset perturbation theory) combined with molecular statistics makes an accurate description of the interactions between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules and MOF-5 possible. We show that quantum effects must be taken into account when calculating adsorption enthalpies. With the method used we can reproduce measured adsorption isotherms if we assume that the adsorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules have preserved their rotational degrees of freedom. The reference data presented here for the ideal structure allow discrimination between different isotherms measured for different MOF-5 samples and reliable prediction of isotherms for new MOF structures.
